{
  "id": "alignment-amoebots",
  "model": "programmable-matter",
  "name": "Alignment",
  "origin": {
    "ref": "Kedia2022Alignment",
    "year": 2022,
    "note": "APPROX/RANDOM 2022."
  },
  "derived_from": {
    "problem": "compression-amoebots",
    "kind": "generalisation of",
    "relation": "Alignment adds an orientation to each particle; the same chains also give compression and expansion [Kedia2022Alignment]."
  },
  "summary": "Oriented particles, following a local stochastic rule, must align their orientations.",
  "statement": "Each particle carries one of $q \\ge 2$ **orientations**, a spin state, not a sense of direction. Particles run a local **Markov chain**; the system should be **aligned** (most particles share an orientation) or not, depending on the **bias**, with high probability in the stationary distribution, both when connectivity is kept and when particles may disconnect.",
  "primer": {
    "intro": "Picture a school of fish or a flock of birds in which every animal turns to face the same way as its neighbours. The floor is the **triangular lattice**: the endless graph whose **nodes** are the corners of a tiling of the plane by equal triangles; each node is joined by an edge to six others, its **neighbours**. A **particle** occupies one node and moves in two actions: an **expansion** into an empty neighbouring node, after which it occupies both, then a **contraction** into one of the two; so one expansion and one contraction move it by one node. The **system** of $n$ particles starts connected, and each particle carries an **orientation**, one of $q \\ge 2$ values drawn as an arrow; the orientation is just a state, not a compass, and a particle can only compare its orientation with its neighbours' orientations. One randomly chosen particle acts at a time; it either steps (moves to a neighbouring node) or turns (changes its orientation). The system should become **aligned** (one orientation is held by most particles) or stay *non-aligned* (all orientations about equally common), depending on a bias; this is studied both when the system must stay connected and when it may fall apart.",
    "ideas": [
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Step or turn, with a bias towards agreeing neighbours",
        "variant": "stochastic",
        "ref": "Kedia2022Alignment",
        "idea": "In the connected setting there are two **biases**: $\\lambda$ rewards having neighbours, as in compression, and a second bias, written $\\gamma$ in [Kedia2022Alignment], rewards **agreeing neighbours** (neighbours with the same orientation). A randomly chosen particle does one of two things, with equal chance.\n\n1. **Step.** Pick a random empty neighbouring node; check locally that the system stays connected without **holes** (regions of empty nodes surrounded by particles); move with a probability that grows with the gain in neighbours and in agreeing neighbours.\n2. **Turn.** Pick a new orientation; accept it with a probability that grows with the gain in agreeing neighbours (certainly, if it gains).",
        "analysis": "With both biases large (for instance $\\gamma > 29.3(q-1)$ and $\\lambda\\gamma$ large enough), the system is **compressed** (its perimeter, the length of the walk around its outside, is at most a constant times the minimum) with high probability, and under further conditions on $\\gamma$ one orientation dominates [Kedia2022Alignment]. With $\\lambda$ large but $\\gamma$ small, the system is compressed but its orientations stay evenly split among the $q$ values; with $\\lambda$ small, the system stays stretched out. Compactness matters: a long thin bottleneck lets two regions with different orientations coexist cheaply, so alignment is proved only for well compressed systems.",
        "why_it_matters": "It brings the Potts and clock models of magnetism into programmable matter, with particles that move as well as turn."
      },
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "When particles may drift apart: one knob",
        "variant": "stochastic",
        "ref": "Kedia2022Alignment",
        "idea": "If the system is allowed to disconnect, the connectivity check is dropped: a particle may step to any empty neighbouring node. A single bias then rewards, at the same time, having neighbours and having agreeing neighbours.\n\n1. Pick step or turn with equal chance.\n2. Step: move to a random empty neighbouring node, accepted with a probability that grows with the gain in (agreeing) neighbours.\n3. Turn: as before.",
        "analysis": "When the single bias is large, the particles gather into compact **clusters** (sets of particles connected through neighbours) and align; when it is small, they disperse and the orientations stay balanced [Kedia2022Alignment]. The proof is harder than in the connected case because a spread-out system can contain many small clusters scattered over the lattice.",
        "why_it_matters": "It shows that the stochastic approach does not need the connectivity rule, which is costly to check and not always realistic."
      }
    ]
  },
  "glossary": [
    {
      "term": "particle",
      "definition": "an anonymous computing unit of constant size that occupies one node of the triangular lattice, or two neighbouring nodes while it moves",
      "avoid": [
        "robot",
        "agent"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "triangular lattice",
      "definition": "the infinite graph $G_\\Delta$ whose nodes are the corners of a tiling of the plane by equilateral triangles; every node has six neighbours",
      "avoid": [
        "grid"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "node",
      "definition": "a point of the triangular lattice; it is empty or occupied by a particle (an expanded particle occupies two)",
      "avoid": [
        "spot",
        "vertex"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "neighbour",
      "definition": "two nodes are neighbours when an edge of the triangular lattice joins them; two particles are neighbours when they occupy neighbouring nodes",
      "avoid": [
        "adjacent",
        "touching"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "expansion",
      "definition": "the action in which a particle that occupies one node extends into an empty neighbouring node and then occupies both"
    },
    {
      "term": "contraction",
      "definition": "the action in which a particle that occupies two nodes withdraws into one of them"
    },
    {
      "term": "system",
      "definition": "the set of all $n$ particles",
      "avoid": [
        "group",
        "swarm"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "hole",
      "definition": "a finite set of empty nodes enclosed by particles"
    },
    {
      "term": "compressed",
      "definition": "having perimeter (the length of the walk around the outside of the system) at most a constant times the minimum possible perimeter of $n$ particles"
    },
    {
      "term": "Markov chain",
      "definition": "the random process in which, at each step, one particle chosen uniformly at random proposes an action and accepts it with a probability set by the biases and its neighbourhood; results describe its stationary distribution"
    },
    {
      "term": "orientation",
      "definition": "the state of a particle, one of $q \\ge 2$ values (a spin, drawn as an arrow or a colour), not a sense of direction on the lattice"
    },
    {
      "term": "agreeing neighbours",
      "definition": "the neighbours of a particle that carry the same orientation as it"
    },
    {
      "term": "aligned",
      "definition": "most particles carry the same orientation"
    },
    {
      "term": "bias",
      "definition": "a parameter of the Markov chain: in the connected setting $\\lambda$ rewards neighbours and $\\gamma$ rewards agreeing neighbours; when the system may disconnect, a single bias rewards both"
    },
    {
      "term": "cluster",
      "definition": "a maximal set of particles connected through neighbours",
      "avoid": [
        "clump"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "history": "Alignment brought Potts and clock models from statistical physics to programmable matter [Kedia2022Alignment].",
